  <h1>Fitting a floor plan to the footprint</h1>
  <p class="meta">Last tended 19 November 2026</p>

  <p>Placeholder copy. A drawn floor plan knows the rooms but not where it sits in the world. The city's footprint polygon knows the true outline in meters but nothing inside it. Fitting one to the other gives the plan real coordinates.</p>
  <ul>
    <li>Scale and rotate the plan's outer walls onto the footprint, per level.</li>
    <li>Anchor on features both sources agree on: corners, the stair, the garage.</li>
    <li>Score the fit as outline overlap. One test house: 0.85, 0.91, 0.93 across three levels.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>The plan's own area came out about 10% smaller than the footprint, which is mostly wall thickness. See <a class="stub" href="#">Public data about a house</a> for where the footprint comes from.</p>
</article>

